  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nWarming Through The Week With Frequent Precipitation Chances:\n\nA cyclonically wrapping extratropical cyclone on upper level \nwater vapor imagery loops slowly trudges east through the start\nof the new week, circumnavigating and exerting influence on a \nsynoptic upper level ridge over the central CONUS, resulting in \nthe local forecast area sandwiched in the accompanying \nconfluence zone. Resultant periodic precipitation and storm \nchances frequent the local forecast area mostly during the \nnighttime hours through the start of the week.\n\nStorm Chances Today:\n\nDaily lowest local confidence (<25%) for storms Today can be\nattributed to high resolution soundings suggesting low level \ndry air causing a capping inversion. Learning from Sunday, when\nstorms were able to initiate under similar model conditions as \nwidespread sky cover throughout the daytime hours limited low \nlevel mixing, resulting in more moist lower levels than \npreviously expected, and high based (8000') storms accessing mid\nlevel instability. Therefore, have veered away from high \nresolution models for Today which again suggest copious mixing \ncausing a strong, dry low level inversion. Degree of \ncooling/moistening of low level inversion will be determining \nfactor in coverage and strength of storms through today. More \nboisterous solutions in previous RAP (30.21Z) and recent HRRR \n(31.00Z) raises local concerns for potentially severe storms in \na very narrow temporal and spatial window within convergent zone\nalong surface frontal boundary. Large hail and damaging winds \nwould be the main threats should severe storms come to fruition.\n\nStorm Chances Through The Week:\n\nConfidence in storms increases through the start of the week,\nfrom Tuesday night as moisture-laden southwest low level flow \nadvects northeast along a meager, lifting upper/mid level \nperturbation behind the exiting synoptic trough axis and again\nWednesday night as a narrow corridor of higher \nstorm/precipitation potential from a burbling low level theta e\nlobe along our southern periphery again advecting northeast.",
